\begin{abstract}
In this paper, in order to address the important and challenging problem of long-horizon flood forecasting from sparse gauges, which has been studied by many works, we propose TideNet, a weight-shared recurrent operator, which uses a residual gate, and which we evaluate on three basins.
Experiments are organized around the core scientific propositions, and the formal main table will be rerun with the finalized model and a unified recipe; early exploratory runs with mixed parameter scales are used only for design diagnosis and no longer enter the main table.
TideNet reaches NSE 0.81 versus 0.74 for ConvLSTM, and at $T=128$ it drops 0.02 while ConvLSTM drops 0.19.
\end{abstract}
